+
+All undated sources were checked on 2026-10-08. Their publication dates are not exposed, so no date has been inferred from a crawl timestamp. Vendor pricing citations establish published standard API rates, not independently audited invoices. The repeated rates in the cost-to-task bullet are covered by P1 through P7. Other rows and unchanged recommendations are outside the brief's factual scope.
+
+**H9: narrow or remove the tool-workflow claim.** Haiku scored 35% versus Luna's 53% on AutomationBench-AA. The evaluator attributes an understatement to a pre-release refusal problem and plans a rerun. This cannot establish general tool-workflow inferiority. Remove the generalization or narrow it to a provisional SaaS-workflow preference. [AA Haiku report](https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/claude-haiku-5-5), independent, 2026-10-07.
+
+**S2: update the directional comparison.** The September 28 analysis reported $7.60 for Sonnet `max`, above Opus's $5.98. The current comparison uses Sonnet's $0.10 cache reads and reports $5.46 against $5.98. The draft's “about the same” survives, but “or more” should not present the older aggregate ranking as current. A precise replacement is: “At max, its measured task cost is close to Opus 5.5.” Specific workloads can still reverse the ordering. [Historical AA analysis](https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/claude-sonnet-5-5), independent, 2026-09-28, and [current AA comparison](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-sonnet-5-5-vs-claude-opus-5-5), independent, undated.
+
+**Qualifications that matter when using the HOLDS verdicts.**
+
+- H7 needs an effort pairing. Equal aggregate scores occur at Haiku `high` versus Luna `max`. Haiku's `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max` scores are 34, 38, 41, and 43. Treat “about level” as a rough class comparison. [AA Haiku release results](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/releases/claude-haiku-5-5), independent, undated.
+- Haiku costs exclude its surcharge and remain provisional. Adding that charge cannot reverse C6, holding Sol's estimate fixed. H10's “often” is supported by benchmark averages, not a measured task frequency. [AA Haiku analysis](https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/claude-haiku-5-5), independent, 2026-10-07.
+- The Sonnet `medium` default is specifically Claude Code behavior. The API defaults Sonnet to `high`. These are compatible facts, not contradictory documentation. [API effort documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort), vendor, undated.
+- L6 describes a relative pricing benefit, not a universal winner. For 300K uncached input tokens and 10K output tokens, standard-rate arithmetic gives Sol 6.1 $1.35, Sonnet $0.70, Opus $1.40, and Fable $3.50. Even beyond the threshold, larger Claude models do not all beat Sol. Real runs also differ in tokenization, generated tokens, caching, and retries. Calculation from [Claude pricing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing) and [Sol documentation](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-sol), vendor, undated. Prefer: “Long inputs improve the larger Claudes' relative pricing; compare the specific models and cache mix.”
+- Benchmark cost per task is not cost per successful completion. The draft's general definition of getting work done should not imply that the quoted averages have been normalized by success rate. [AA methodology explanation](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-haiku-5-5-vs-gpt-6-1-sol-low), independent, undated.
+
+**Ratings.** I have not assigned verdicts to the depth, execution, or cost ratings. No scoped rating is clearly disproved at the recommended routine effort. Haiku's “lowest” cost category becomes misleading if readers carry it to `max` or long contexts, which the row already warns about. Identical execution ratings for Haiku and Luna hide their opposite terminal/SaaS strengths, but a coarse rating is not inherently wrong. The research supports naming the effort and workload rather than changing the numeric scores on this evidence alone.
+
+**Material to remove from the shipped draft.** The Haiku sentence “Newly released, so this placement rests on thin early evidence” explicitly describes the evidence behind the document. Remove that sentence under the brief's rule. The release date is independently verifiable, but “thin” describes research confidence rather than model behavior. I found no named sources, named studies, “this used to say” history, or explicit notes about honesty, evaluation gaming, outages, or silent substitution anywhere in the supplied draft. The warning about returning no visible output is a runtime-behavior warning, not an outage or substitution claim. Its factual verification was outside the changed scope.
+
+**Source concentration and access limits.** Almost all independent performance and cost verdicts rest on one evaluator, Artificial Analysis. Its many model and comparison URLs are not independent replications of one another. Vendor documents are the only support used for exact CLI defaults, product availability, and several workload recommendations. The launch page's customer quotations were not counted as separate independent sources. No live API calls or independent benchmark runs were performed.
+
+Web search worked. Some direct page opens failed, including the Haiku overview, its initially attempted prompting URL, AA's Haiku article and German variant, and a Haiku-medium/Luna-medium comparison URL. The English AA article, official overview, and correct prompting page were subsequently retrieved through working links. The German page and medium/medium comparison were not recovered or relied on. The draft was not edited.

